# The audioif floor. Every gate in this repository runs against this exact
# audioif commit: the components are held to a fixed core the same way
# audioif itself is held to a fixed CircuitPython (its CIRCUITPYTHON_ORACLE).
#
# A floating core makes a component failure unattributable -- you cannot tell
# a rewritten instrument from a moved node underneath it. Moving this pin is
# its own change, with the gates re-run and the goldens re-read, never a
# side effect of some other work.
#
# This pin names an UNRELEASED commit rather than a release tag, deliberately:
# audioif carries four synthio fixes that the accuracy program needs -- the
# CPython press semantics and filter cascade (b420dac), a re-pressed finished
# note being dropped entirely (afb6b71, also reported upstream), ring
# modulation, which the CPython target had never implemented (52a1d4e) and
# which three Phase 2 instruments depend on, and envelope reassignment not
# being live on the CPython target (d84470a). The release waits until the bug
# hunt settles; the floor did not need to.
#
# Moved to d84470a on 2026-09-02. That fix is what makes one circuit shared by
# two voices with different decays behave correctly under CPython, as it
# already did on MicroPython and the oracle: before it, a closed hat struck
# 250 ms after an open hat rang for 400 ms instead of 53 ms. It changes the
# rendered sound of tr808 and cr78, whose hats share a circuit, so both were
# re-listened.
#
